---
layout: layouts/feature.njk
wide: true
title: Authentifizierung | Shellui
description: "Teile Sign-in und Sessions über deine Shellui-Apps. Verbinde identity-service oder Supabase Auth, schütze Navigation und verwalte Company Access."
eyebrow: Funktionen
heading: "Ein Sign-in-Flow für deine Apps"
lede: "Konfiguriere Sign-in einmal in Shellui. Die Shell übernimmt Login, Session-Erneuerung und die Rückkehr zur angeforderten Seite. Eingebettete Apps erhalten das angemeldete Konto über das SDK. Wähle identity-service oder verbinde ein bestehendes Supabase-Auth-Projekt."
pageScripts:
  - /assets/js/auth-flow.js
---

<section aria-labelledby="auth-exchange">
  <div class="max-w-2xl">
    <h2 id="auth-exchange" class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground sm:text-3xl">How sign-in works</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base/7 text-muted-foreground">With identity-service, you can sign in using a GitHub, Google, or Microsoft account through OAuth, an authorization protocol. An embedded app calls <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm">shellui.login()</code> to start the flow in the main browser window. After sign-in and account confirmation, identity-service returns the session to the shell.</p>
  </div>
  {% include "auth-flow.njk" %}
</section>

<section class="mt-20" aria-labelledby="identity-service-does">
  <div class="max-w-2xl">
    <h2 id="identity-service-does" class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground sm:text-3xl">Choose an authentication backend</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base/7 text-muted-foreground">The <a class="font-medium text-primary-ink underline underline-offset-2 dark:text-primary" href="{{ site.identityService }}">identity-service</a> backend checks who you are and whether you can access a company. A company groups the accounts and access rules for your product or organization. Use it for the company access features below, or choose Supabase Auth for an existing Supabase project.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="mt-10 grid gap-10 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">OAuth providers</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm/6 text-muted-foreground">Connect GitHub, Google, or Microsoft as sign-in providers. Configure provider credentials for each company on identity-service.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">Signed session tokens</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm/6 text-muted-foreground">identity-service issues JSON Web Tokens (JWTs) for access and session renewal. In production, it signs tokens with an RSA key and publishes a JSON Web Key Set (JWKS) so your services can verify them.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">Company access rules</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm/6 text-muted-foreground">Choose open registration, approved email domains, or owner approval. Access applies per company, so the same account can have different memberships.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">Approved return addresses</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm/6 text-muted-foreground">Register the provider callback on identity-service and approve the addresses your shell can return to after sign-in. The authentication guide covers both settings.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">Account administration</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm/6 text-muted-foreground">Staff and company owners can manage members, groups, and provider connections in the administration panel.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">Supabase Auth</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm/6 text-muted-foreground">If you already use Supabase, connect it as your authentication backend. Available sign-in methods depend on your backend settings; Supabase also supports email magic links.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-20" aria-labelledby="connect-and-guard">
  <div class="max-w-2xl">
    <h2 id="connect-and-guard" class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground sm:text-3xl">Connect a backend and guard routes</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base/7 text-muted-foreground">Connect your authentication backend and mark navigation items with <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm">requiresAuth</code>. Shellui asks signed-out visitors to sign in before loading those apps, then returns them to the requested page.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-10 grid max-w-3xl gap-6 sm:grid-cols-3">
    <div>
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-primary-ink dark:text-primary">1. Connect a backend</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm/6 text-muted-foreground">Set <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-[0.8rem]">backend</code> in <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-[0.8rem]">shellui.config.json</code> and enable the login methods you want on the page.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-primary-ink dark:text-primary">2. Protect routes</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm/6 text-muted-foreground">Set <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm">requiresAuth</code> on the navigation items that need a session. Your backend must also check authentication and permissions for its data.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-primary-ink dark:text-primary">3. Sign in from your app</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm/6 text-muted-foreground">From an embedded app, call <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 text-[0.8rem]">shellui.login()</code> so the shell can start the redirect.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl">
    {% highlight "json", "shellui.config.json" %}
{
  "backend": {
    "type": "shellui",
    "url": "http://localhost:8000",
    "companyId": 1,
    "login": {
      "methods": ["oauth"],
      "oauthProviders": ["github", "google"]
    }
  },
  "navigation": [
    {
      "label": "Billing",
      "path": "billing",
      "url": "https://app.example.com/billing",
      "requiresAuth": true
    }
  ]
}
    {% endhighlight %}
  </div>
</section>

{# Future screenshot: Playground login. Add a real image here when available. #}

<section class="mt-20" aria-labelledby="who-can-join">
  <div class="max-w-2xl">
    <h2 id="who-can-join" class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground sm:text-3xl">Choose who can join</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base/7 text-muted-foreground">A successful provider login confirms an account's identity. Your company access policy decides whether that account can enter your product. Choose a policy in the administration panel's Organization settings.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 md:grid-cols-3 md:gap-6 lg:gap-8">
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">Public</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm/6 text-muted-foreground">Anyone who signs in can join with access enabled. Use this mode when registration is open to everyone.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">Approved email domains</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm/6 text-muted-foreground">Accounts with an approved email domain join with access enabled. Other accounts wait for approval, and company owners receive a notification.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">Invitation only</h3>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm/6 text-muted-foreground">New accounts wait for a staff member or company owner to enable their membership. They cannot receive a session for that company while approval is pending.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

</section>

<section class="mt-20" aria-labelledby="groups-logs-tokens">
  <div class="max-w-2xl">
    <h2 id="groups-logs-tokens" class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground sm:text-3xl">Manage accounts beyond sign-in</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base/7 text-muted-foreground">Manage company groups, review login events, and create access tokens for integrations in the administration panel. These tools use the same identity-service backend as sign-in.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base/7 text-muted-foreground">Your APIs can use group information from verified tokens when checking access. Named personal access tokens can be revoked, and read-only tokens are available for integrations that only fetch data.</p>
  </div>
</section>

